;; =============================================================================
;; Spec: In-flight PR / branch / task-claim registry
;; =============================================================================
;;
;; Observed failure mode (2026-05-22, PRs #953-#962):
;;   Across 10 PRs in one day, the planner / DAG decomposer kept fanning the
;;   same root cause ("artifact-session false ERROR / WARN") into sibling
;;   PRs that produced near-identical diffs. Concrete evidence on #961
;;   vs #962: shared base commit 8d8b2589, byte-identical 105-line
;;   `artifact_session.clj` diffs vs main, distinguished only by leaf
;;   commits + PR doc. Same pattern on 954/956 and 955/957/958.
;;
;; Root cause (mapped during design 2026-05-22):
;;   Producers of branches and PRs (release-executor inside DAG tasks) have
;;   no shared, durable, write-as-you-go registry that other producers can
;;   read or be notified by. Closest existing primitive is the per-workflow
;;   `dag-executor/branch_registry.clj`, which is pull-based and populated
;;   only POST-task-completion — sibling tasks running in parallel cannot
;;   see each other's claims while they run, and concurrent workflow
;;   invocations cannot see each other at all. "Active context of what's
;;   in PR" doesn't exist as a primitive. GitHub is the only durable
;;   surface, but no in-process component queries it at runtime, and it
;;   doesn't carry our intent metadata.
;;
;; This is not just a duplication problem — it's also a work-recovery
;; problem. Today, when a workflow dies mid-task, the task is orphaned;
;; with a claim FSM that times out, another workflow can re-acquire from
;; the existing `:workspace/persisted` checkpoint. Crash-resilient
;; distributed task execution falls out as a side-effect.
;;
;; Related memory:
;;   project_in_flight_blind_spot.md
;;   project_autonomous_pr_shepherd_gap.md
;;   project_autonomous_pr_stack_drift.md

{:spec/title "In-flight PR / branch / task-claim registry"

 :spec/description
 "Build a read-through / write-through registry that mediates all PR and
  branch state between miniforge agents and the upstream Git host
  (GitHub / GitLab). The registry is the single seam between local agent
  activity and external state, and doubles as a distributed claim system
  that prevents duplicate sibling PRs AND enables work recovery when a
  workflow dies mid-task.

  Architecture (layered cache):

    L1: orchestrator-local ref / atom (per-workflow, in-process)
        ─ fast intra-DAG visibility; siblings see each other's claims
          and facts immediately without IO

    L2: Datalevin transactions (per-repo, cross-process)
        ─ durable across workflow invocations on the same machine
        ─ Datalevin's ACID transactions ARE the STM substrate; first
          write to commit wins, later writer yields cleanly

    L3: GitHub / GitLab (authoritative for landed PRs)
        ─ read-through on L2 miss or staleness; truth when the cache
          and the host disagree

  Identity: a task-level snowflake (own generator, NOT Fleet's spec-level
  one) encoding spec-id, intent-hash, and a monotonic counter. Time-
  sortable, collision-safe, decodable.

  Claim FSM:
      nil ──claim──▶ :claimed ──start──▶ :working ──complete──▶ :finished
                       │                    │
                     TTL│                   ├──abort──▶ :failed
                       ▼                    │              │
                  (cleared,                 └──liveness────┴──▶ (cleared,
                   re-acquirable                signal lost      checkpoint
                   from checkpoint)             via heuristics    recoverable)

  Claims are issued by the planner at fan-out (one per task in the DAG).
  TTL on :claimed is short — only the gap between 'claimed' and 'started
  working.' Once :working, the claim is held until :finished, :failed,
  or a liveness signal clears it. For V1, liveness piggybacks on existing
  stall-detection heuristics (orchestrator stagnation timeouts, kill-
  workflow signals). A future real liveness protocol plugs into the same
  surface via `:claim/heartbeat-source`.

  Write-through happens via the existing artifact-session promotion
  channel (container / worktree / MCP) — release becomes 'submit a
  release-claim artifact', the registry observes the artifact and writes
  branch + PR via the existing release-executor `gh` shell-out, records
  the fact, invalidates the claim. Same shape as `.miniforge/plan.edn`
  and `.miniforge/release.edn` promotion.

  Conflict resolution at claim time: first Datalevin transaction commits
  wins; loser yields and its task either converts to a 'wait for PR #X'
  task (downstream of the winner) or is dropped if redundant. Snowflake
  order is informational metadata, not the arbiter.

  Coalescent workflow (M4): when two facts converge on the same intent —
  e.g., two open PRs whose intent-hash matches and whose diffs overlap
  significantly — run an LLM-driven coalescer that selects the best
  ideas from each and produces a single consolidated PR.

  Cross-process notify: leverage the existing transit-on-disk evidence
  bundle channel (already used to surface workflow state to TUI/consoles)
  + optional websocket. Local-first, no GitHub pub-sub / webhook setup.

  MILESTONES

  GROUP 1 — Snowflake generator + Datalevin schema + read-through (M1)
  New component `pr-registry`. Implements:
    - `ai.miniforge.pr-registry.snowflake/gen-id` — task-level snowflake.
      Bit layout TBD-in-impl; carries spec-id, intent-hash, counter.
    - `ai.miniforge.pr-registry.schema` — Datalevin schema for registry
      entries. Schema is mutable for V1; freeze after first dogfood use.
    - `ai.miniforge.pr-registry.interface/lookup-by-intent` and
      `/lookup-by-branch` — read-only, read-through to GitHub via
      existing `pr-sync/core.clj:220-245` functions on L2 miss.
    - Adopt knowledge-store's Datalevin instance as the L2 backing store
      (already part of every miniforge invocation; no new dependency).
  No writes yet — V1 is observation-only so we can dogfood it without
  affecting PR creation.

  GROUP 2 — Claim FSM + write-through via artifact-session (M2)
  - Add `:claim` artifact type to `components/agent/src/.../artifact-
    session.clj` system-message-templates / role list. Promoted the same
    way `:plan`, `:implement`, etc. are.
  - `ai.miniforge.pr-registry.claim` implements the FSM:
    {:claim/id snowflake-id
     :claim/spec-id … :claim/intent-hash …
     :claim/state #{:claimed :working :finished :failed}
     :claim/workflow-id … :claim/task-id …
     :claim/claimed-at … :claim/started-at … :claim/finished-at …
     :claim/expires-at …
     :claim/heartbeat-source :heuristic/stall-detector  ; pluggable
     :claim/branch (when present) :claim/pr-number (when present)
     :claim/last-seen-github …}
  - Write path: `release-executor/sandbox.clj:205` `create-pr!` calls
    `pr-registry/record-pr!` before / after the shell-out so the fact
    lands in L2 transactionally.
  - L1 ref in `dag-executor` orchestrator mirrors L2 for the current
    workflow run.
  - Liveness: wire claim expiry to existing stall-detector signals
    (workflow-runner kill-workflow, progress-monitor timeouts). No new
    heartbeat protocol.

  GROUP 3 — Planner + release dedup consumers (M3)
  - Planner (`components/agent/src/.../planner.clj`): after `parse-plan-
    response` but before plan emission, consult `pr-registry/lookup-by-
    intent` for each candidate task's intent-hash. If a winning claim
    exists, replace the task with either a 'wait for PR #X' marker or
    drop it (if the existing PR is already open and the planner verifies
    it covers the intent).
  - Release-executor (`release-executor/core.clj` `step-create-branch`):
    consult `pr-registry/lookup-by-intent` and `lookup-by-branch` before
    pushing. On match, yield with a structured anomaly so the orchestrator
    can re-target the task downstream of the winning PR.
  - Workflow-resume (`workflow-resume/core.clj:302`): consult registry
    on resume to learn what landed during the pause; mark affected tasks
    as already-implemented / superseded.

  GROUP 4 — Coalescent workflow (M4)
  Triggered when PR-shepherd (or any background sweep) observes two
  open PRs whose claims share intent-hash AND whose diffs overlap by
  more than a configurable threshold. New workflow type `:coalesce-prs`
  that:
    - Loads both PR diffs + review threads.
    - Invokes a coalescer LLM (Sonnet, reviewer-shaped) that selects
      the better implementation per area and produces a unified diff
      + rationale.
    - Opens a new PR for the unified diff, closes the two source PRs
      with a 'superseded by #N' comment, transfers any unresolved review
      threads.
  V1 is opt-in — PR-shepherd flags candidates but a human triggers
  coalescence until we trust the selector.

  GROUP 5 — Cross-process notify via evidence bundle + websocket (M5)
  - Every registry transition (claim → working → finished, PR opened,
    PR closed) emits a transit event onto the existing evidence bundle
    channel under `.miniforge/registry/<snowflake>.transit`.
  - TUI / dashboard subscribers (already polling evidence bundles) pick
    these up automatically; no new wire-up.
  - Optional: websocket emit for low-latency dashboard updates, reusing
    the existing dashboard websocket surface.
  - Off-box (Fleet): the transit-on-disk channel is already the surface
    Fleet observes, so cross-machine visibility comes along for free."

 :spec/intent {:type :feature
               :scope ["components/pr-registry/src"
                       "components/pr-registry/test"
                       "components/agent/src/ai/miniforge/agent/artifact_session.clj"
                       "components/agent/src/ai/miniforge/agent/planner.clj"
                       "components/release-executor/src/ai/miniforge/release_executor"
                       "components/dag-executor/src/ai/miniforge/dag_executor/orchestrator.clj"
                       "components/workflow-resume/src"
                       "components/pr-sync/src"]}

 :spec/constraints
 ["L1 (orchestrator ref) is workflow-scoped and never durable; every claim
   write goes through L2 (Datalevin) transactionally before L1 mirrors it"
  "L2 (Datalevin) is the cross-process source of truth for in-flight claims;
   GitHub is the cross-process source of truth for landed PRs"
  "Datalevin transactions are the conflict resolver — never implement a
   separate first-write-wins shim; rely on the underlying ACID semantics"
  "Snowflake generator is our own (task-level granularity), borrowing
   Fleet's shape but not its bit layout"
  "Claim TTL on :claimed is short (default 5min) — long enough to start
   working, short enough that abandoned claims free up quickly"
  "Liveness signal for :working is pluggable via :claim/heartbeat-source;
   V1 wires to existing stall-detection heuristics, do not invent a new
   heartbeat protocol in this spec"
  "Write-through must reuse the artifact-session promotion channel —
   container / worktree / MCP; do not introduce a parallel pipeline"
  "No GitHub pub-sub / webhook in V1; cross-process notify uses the
   existing transit-on-disk evidence bundle channel"
  "Read-through to GitHub must be TTL-bounded so we don't hammer the
   host on every consult; default 30s for hot intents, 5min for cold"
  "M1 is observation-only — no consumer can yield on the registry until
   M2 + M3 land; this lets us dogfood the read path before risking
   behaviour change"
  "Schema mutability: explicit V1 freedom to evolve the Datalevin schema
   in-place; freeze after first full dogfood run validates the shape"
  "Tests must include simulated concurrent-workflow scenarios that prove
   first-Datalevin-transaction wins and the loser yields cleanly"]

 :spec/acceptance-criteria
 ["M1: `pr-registry/lookup-by-intent` returns the correct entry for an
   open PR on GitHub via read-through; misses populate L2 transactionally"
  "M1: Snowflake IDs are time-sortable, decodable into spec-id +
   intent-hash + counter, and unique across 10⁴ concurrent generations"
  "M2: Claim FSM transitions are atomic via Datalevin transactions;
   simultaneous claim attempts on the same intent — exactly one commits,
   the other yields without retry-storm"
  "M2: release-executor's `create-pr!` records the fact in L2 within
   the same transaction boundary; no PR exists on GitHub without a
   corresponding L2 fact entry"
  "M3: Planner consults the registry at fan-out; a duplicate-intent task
   is replaced with a marker referencing the existing PR (visible in
   the plan artifact)"
  "M3: Release-executor consults the registry before push; a duplicate
   branch / intent yields with a structured anomaly the orchestrator can
   handle"
  "M3: Workflow-resume consults the registry on resume; tasks whose
   intent landed during the pause are marked already-implemented"
  "M4: Two open PRs with overlapping diffs + matching intent-hash are
   flagged by PR-shepherd; the coalescent workflow produces a unified
   PR and closes the two sources (opt-in trigger)"
  "M5: Registry transitions emit transit-on-disk events under
   `.miniforge/registry/`; TUI and dashboard subscribers see them
   without code changes"
  "Crash-recovery: a workflow killed during :working leaves a claim that
   times out (via liveness signal) and is re-acquired by a fresh
   workflow from the last `:workspace/persisted` checkpoint"
  "Existing dogfood workflows do not regress with M1 installed (read-only)"
  "Two concurrent dogfood invocations targeting the same intent — exactly
   one ships a PR, the other yields with telemetry"]

 :spec/priority
 {:tier :high
  :axes #{:dogfood-enabler :token-conservation :resilience}
  :theme :orchestration
  :rationale
  "Closes the in-flight blind spot that produced the 953-962 duplicate-PR
   cluster (10 PRs in one day, several pairs byte-identical on the source
   diff). Also unlocks crash-resilient task execution as a side-effect —
   workflows that die mid-task become recoverable from checkpoint instead
   of orphaned. The L1/L2/L3 cache architecture composes with existing
   primitives (artifact-session promotion, Datalevin knowledge store,
   evidence-bundle transit channel) — no new infrastructure introduced."
  :blocks ["autonomous-pr-shepherd.spec.edn"]
  :blocked-by []}

 :spec/workflow-type :canonical-sdlc}
